1
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:15,250
Hey guys, welcome back to our IELTS listening course.

2
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:18,870
Have you ever felt confident that you heard the right answer, wrote

3
00:00:18,870 --> 00:00:21,130
it down immediately, but still got the question wrong?

4
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:25,010
If so, you have likely fallen into a trap known as a distractor.

5
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:28,750
A distractor is a piece of information that looks or sounds like

6
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,810
the correct answer, but is actually wrong.

7
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:37,650
IELTS uses these to test if you are listening for meaning, rather

8
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:39,270
than just matching keywords.

9
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:44,790
This brings us to the first major type of trap, the partial match.

10
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:55,260
Please note that, when given all choices, you are likely to hear

11
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:57,240
the speaker talking about all options.

12
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:01,900
Just because you hear it mentioned doesn't make it correct, so be

13
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:05,080
careful to analyze the choices and choose the correct answer.

14
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:10,200
In a multiple-choice question, you might see options like blue, red, and green.

15
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,780
The recording won't just say, the answer is red.

16
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,220
Instead, the speaker will mention all three.

17
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:21,200
They might say, I originally wanted blue, and green was too bright, so we finally decided on red.

18
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:26,000
If you stop listening, the moment you hear a keyword, you will fail.

19
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:32,000
You must listen to the context to understand which option is being accepted and which are being rejected.

20
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:34,420
But listing options isn't the only trick.

21
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:36,840
The second major trap is the self-correction.

22
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:41,080
This happens when the speaker gives you an answer, but then immediately changes their mind.

23
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:45,220
Remember, don't rush to write the first answer you hear.

24
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,780
It could be a distractor.

25
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:51,320
Sometimes the speaker only discusses before giving the final decision.

26
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:54,060
And that's the correct answer.

27
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:57,920
This rush to write is dangerous because of signal words.

28
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:04,420
Distractors are almost always followed by contrast markers like but, however, actually, or in fact.

29
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,259
Imagine the question asks for a meeting time.

30
00:02:07,479 --> 00:02:09,220
You hear, shall we meet at 5pm?

31
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:11,680
You might write down 5pm.

32
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:15,500
But then the speaker says, actually, I have a dentist appointment then.

33
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:16,940
Let's make it 6 o'clock.

34
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:19,600
The first time, 5 o'clock was the distractor.

35
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:21,980
The signal word actually cancelled it.

36
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:26,080
The final time, 6 o'clock was the correct answer.

37
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,600
So the golden rule for this lesson is patience.

38
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,320
Never trust the first thing you hear.

39
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:37,700
Keep your pencil moving, but don't finalize your answer until the speaker has finished their thought.

40
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:46,300
Now, you might think these traps only apply to multiple choice questions.

41
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:51,260
But actually, IELTS uses these exact same tricks, signal words, corrections,

42
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:54,160
and negatives to confuse you in matching questions.

43
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:59,180
In a matching task, you aren't just choosing one answer, you're connecting two lists.

44
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:02,760
And this is where the negation trap becomes dangerous.

45
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:06,540
I see you have a great gym listed here.

46
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:08,300
Do you also have a swimming pool?

47
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:14,680
We used to have a pool on the roof, but unfortunately, it is currently closed for renovations.

48
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:17,180
However, guests are free to use the sauna.

49
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:22,740
If your task was to match hotel facilities to availability, and you

50
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:28,320
saw swimming pool on the list, your brain naturally wants to tick it off as soon as you hear the word.

51
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:29,340
But notice the trap.

52
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:35,360
The speaker mentioned the pool, but immediately negated it with closed for renovations.

53
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,820
The correct match would likely be the sauna.

54
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:46,040
This brings us to the next level of difficulty, common in section 3 of the listening test, the agreement trap.

55
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:50,360
I think we should focus our presentation on global warming.

56
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:52,320
There is so much data available.

57
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:53,880
I'm not sure.

58
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:55,560
Everyone chooses that.

59
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:58,120
What about ocean pollution instead?

60
00:03:58,820 --> 00:03:59,340
Hmm.

61
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,680
Actually, you're right.

62
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:02,820
Ocean pollution would be more unique.

63
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:03,840
Let's go with that.

64
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:08,120
In matching questions, where you must link a student to a topic,

65
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:11,580
the partial match we discussed earlier appears as a debate.

66
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:14,540
The male student suggested global warming with confidence.

67
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:18,140
If you matched him to that topic immediately, you fell into the trap.

68
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:20,839
You must wait for the consensus.

69
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:23,000
It isn't about who speaks first.

70
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:25,080
It's about who agrees last.

71
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:29,480
Finally, let's look at how the correction trap appears when matching times or dates.

72
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:33,460
Okay, so a table for four?

73
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:40,000
I have an opening this Saturday at 7 p.m. Oh, wait, my flight lands late Saturday.

74
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,700
Is it possible to move that to Sunday instead?

75
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:44,400
Let me check.

76
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:47,180
Yes, Sunday at 7 works.

77
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,160
Here you are matching the booking to the day.

78
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:54,940
The speaker suggested Saturday, but the host corrected it to Sunday.

79
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,580
So here is your strategy for matching questions.

80
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,040
First, don't match the keyword immediately.

81
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:03,660
Just because you hear pool or Saturday doesn't mean it's the answer.

82
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:08,060
Second, listen for the negation.

83
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:12,920
Words like not, unfortunately, or closed.

84
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:15,740
Finally, wait for the agreement.

85
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:19,520
Ensure both speakers have settled on the final decision.

86
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:24,680
By mastering these three traps, self-correction, negation, and agreement, you

87
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:28,860
will stop falling for distractors and start catching the right answers.

88
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:37,960
Now we arrive at the final boss level, pick from a list.

89
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:44,700
This is where IELTS combines all the traps we just learned, negation, agreement, and correction into a single conversation.

90
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:49,300
In these questions, you must select two or three correct answers from a longer list.

91
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:54,060
This is difficult because the speakers will likely discuss every single item on that list.

92
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:56,140
Let's look at a constraint trap.

93
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:03,960
This is when an answer is technically possible, but a specific condition, like cost, time, or rules, makes it wrong.

94
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:08,600
Alright, so what kind of meat dishes should we have?

95
00:06:08,960 --> 00:06:11,000
Some beef wellington would be great.

96
00:06:11,620 --> 00:06:13,040
You are joking, right?

97
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:15,060
That would be quite expensive.

98
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:16,900
Yes, yes, I know.

99
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:18,160
No beef then.

100
00:06:18,490 --> 00:06:22,180
How about something with pork and then another dish with chicken?

101
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:24,400
Okay, that sounds like a good idea.

102
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:27,120
Did you catch the logic flow there?

103
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:28,740
First, you heard beef.

104
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:31,840
If you were just matching keywords, you would have ticked it immediately.

105
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:33,640
But then came the constraint trap.

106
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,820
Oscar mentioned it was too expensive.

107
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:37,020
Then came the negation.

108
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:38,980
Walt explicitly said no beef.

109
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:43,260
After that, they finally proposed the correct answers, pork and chicken.

110
00:06:43,460 --> 00:06:47,100
So for pick from a list questions, think of yourself as a judge in a courtroom.

111
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:50,260
Don't write down the verdict until you have heard all the arguments.

112
00:06:55,730 --> 00:06:57,550
To recap what we learned this lesson.

113
00:06:57,810 --> 00:07:00,510
One, beware of partial matches in multiple choice.

114
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:03,130
Two, listen for negation in matching questions.

115
00:07:03,490 --> 00:07:06,090
Three, watch out for constraints in pick from a list.

116
00:07:06,290 --> 00:07:09,710
Master these three habits and those distractors won't distract you anymore.

117
00:07:09,890 --> 00:07:10,870
See you in the next lesson.

